          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-of/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 3,
          name: "Standard Refrigerator Dimensions",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/standard-refrigerator-dimensions/",
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What are standard refrigerator dimensions?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Most full-size refrigerators are about 28 to 36 inches wide, 61 to 72 inches tall and 28 to 36 inches deep including doors and handles. Top-freezers are usually 30 inches wide and the shortest; French doors are usually 36 inches wide and the tallest. These are typical ranges, so always confirm the exact model's spec sheet before you buy."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What is the standard width of a refrigerator?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "The most common widths are 30, 33 and 36 inches. Top-freezers and many bottom-freezers are 30 inches, many bottom-freezers are 33, and most French doors and side-by-sides are 36. Apartment models are often 24 inches and some top-freezers are 28. Measure your opening and buy a model slightly narrower than it."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How deep is a counter-depth refrigerator?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Counter-depth refrigerators are typically about 27 to 31 inches deep including the doors and handles, with a cabinet box closer to 24 or 25 inches. Standard-depth models are typically around 30 to 36 inches. Makers measure depth in different ways, so compare the same figure, such as depth with doors, across models."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How much space do you need around a refrigerator?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Many spec sheets ask for about half an inch at the sides and top and about an inch at the back for ventilation. Some models need more, and doors may need extra room on the hinge side to open fully. Check the installation guide for your exact model and plan the opening with those gaps included."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How tall is a standard refrigerator?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Most top-freezers are about 61 to 69 inches tall, bottom-freezers about 67 to 70, and French doors and side-by-sides about 68 to 72. Some spec sheets list height to the hinge cap separately, which can add a fraction of an inch. If a cabinet sits above the opening, plan with the tallest listed figure plus the top clearance."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What size refrigerator do I need for a family of four?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "A commonly cited rule of thumb is about 4 to 6 cubic feet per adult, which puts a family of four somewhere around 20 to 25 cubic feet. Households that cook a lot or buy in bulk may want more. Check that the larger refrigerator still fits your opening, doorways and walkway before choosing on capacity."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Will a refrigerator fit through a 30-inch door?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Many will, but not all. A refrigerator usually goes through a doorway on its depth, so compare the depth without doors and handles with the clear width of the doorway. Removing the doors and handles often gains several inches. Measure every doorway, hallway and turn on the delivery path, not just the kitchen opening."
          }
        }
      ],
    },
