                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Counter-depth (any style)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">24 to 36 in.</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">68 to 72 in.</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 27 to 31 in.</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 12 to 23 cu. ft.</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Top-Freezer</h3>

          <p>
            Top-freezers are the shortest and narrowest of the full-size styles. Most are 30 inches wide, though 28-inch models exist for older kitchens. The GE GTE18GTNRWW, for example, is listed at 28 inches wide, 67 3/8 inches tall and 32 5/8 inches deep, while Frigidaire's FFTR1835VW is 30 inches wide and about 66 3/8 inches tall with its hinge.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Bottom-Freezer</h3>

          <p>
            Bottom-freezers usually come in 30-inch and 33-inch widths and run a little taller than top-freezers. Whirlpool's 33-inch WRB322DMBM is listed at 70 inches tall, 32 5/8 inches wide and 33 3/8 inches deep, a useful example of how a few extra inches of width add capacity.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Side-by-Side</h3>

          <p>
            Side-by-sides start around 33 inches wide and most are 36. Because each door is only half the width of the cabinet, they need less room in front to open, which makes them practical in narrow galley kitchens even though the cabinet itself is wide.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">French Door</h3>

          <p>
            French doors are the tallest common style, often around 70 inches, and most are 36 inches wide. Bosch's 800 Series counter-depth French door, for instance, is listed at about 72 inches tall and just under 36 inches wide. Narrower 30- and 33-inch French doors exist for smaller openings.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="width" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Standard Refrigerator Width: 30, 33 and 36 Inches
          </h2>

          <p>
            Width is the dimension most likely to rule a refrigerator out, because the cabinet opening is fixed and a refrigerator cannot be squeezed. Kitchens are typically built around one of three openings, and the refrigerator you buy needs to be slightly narrower than the one you have.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">24 and 28 Inches</h3>

          <p>
            Apartment-size refrigerators are often 24 inches wide, and some full-size top-freezers are 28 inches. These suit small apartments, condos, older homes and secondary spaces. Capacity drops quickly at these widths, so check the cubic feet as well as the width.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">30 Inches</h3>

          <p>
            Thirty inches is the most common width for top-freezer and bottom-freezer refrigerators and a frequent width for older kitchen openings. Many 30-inch models are actually a fraction under 30 inches, such as the Amana ART318FFDS at about 29 3/4 inches, which leaves room for the small side gap the maker asks for.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">33 Inches</h3>

          <p>
            Thirty-three inches is a middle size found on many bottom-freezers and some side-by-sides. It gives noticeably more room than a 30-inch model but will not fit into a 30-inch opening, a common mistake when people upgrade.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">36 Inches</h3>

          <p>
            Thirty-six inches is the standard width for French door and most side-by-side refrigerators in newer kitchens. If your opening is 36 inches, you have the widest choice of styles; if it is narrower, you are limited to a smaller French door or a different style.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="depth" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Counter-Depth vs Standard Depth: About Six Inches of Difference
          </h2>

          <p>
            Depth is where the most confusion comes from, because makers measure it in different ways and because counter-depth refrigerators are marketed as a size class of their own. Kitchen countertops are typically about 24 to 25 inches deep, and the refrigerator depth you see listed is almost always deeper than that.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Standard Depth</h3>

          <p>
            A standard-depth refrigerator is typically around 30 to 36 inches deep including the doors and handles. That means it sticks out several inches past the front of the counters. You get more cubic feet for the money, but the refrigerator dominates the room and can crowd a narrow walkway.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Counter-Depth</h3>

          <p>
            A counter-depth refrigerator has a shallower cabinet, often around 24 to 25 inches for the box itself, and about 27 to 31 inches deep once the doors and handles are added. The doors still stand slightly proud of the counters so they can swing open, but the result looks built in and frees up floor space.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How Depth Is Measured</h3>

          <p>
            Spec sheets may give depth without doors, with doors, with handles, or with the door open. Frigidaire's FFTR1835VW, for example, lists a cabinet depth of 26 5/8 inches but about 30 3/8 inches with the door. When you compare models, compare the same measurement, and use the depth with doors and handles to plan your walkway.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Capacity Trade-Off</h3>

          <p>
            Counter-depth models give up interior space to get that flush look. A counter-depth French door may hold several cubic feet less than a standard-depth model of the same width. If you shop a lot or have a large household, check the cubic feet before you choose the shallower cabinet.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="height" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Refrigerator Height and Overhead Cabinets
          </h2>

          <p>
            Height matters most when the refrigerator sits under a cabinet or soffit. Many kitchens have a cabinet over the refrigerator opening, and a model that is an inch too tall will not slide in.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Typical Heights</h3>

          <p>
            Most top-freezers are about 61 to 69 inches tall, most bottom-freezers about 67 to 70, and most French doors and side-by-sides about 68 to 72. Within a single brand, larger capacity usually means a taller cabinet.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Hinge Caps and Leveling Legs</h3>

          <p>
            Some spec sheets list height to the top of the cabinet and a separate height to the top of the door hinge. Frigidaire, for example, lists 65 7/8 inches without the hinge and 66 3/8 inches with it on the FFTR1835VW. Leveling legs can also add a fraction of an inch. Plan with the taller figure.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Room Above the Refrigerator</h3>

          <p>
            Makers commonly ask for a small gap above the cabinet so heat can escape and so the refrigerator can be rolled in and out. Whirlpool and Amana spec sheets, for example, suggest about half an inch at the top. Check the exact model, because some need more.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="cubic-feet" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Cubic Feet vs Size: How Much Refrigerator Do You Need?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Cubic feet measure the space inside the refrigerator, not the space it takes up in your kitchen. Two refrigerators with the same outside dimensions can have different capacities, and two with the same capacity can have very different footprints.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">A Rough Rule of Thumb</h3>

          <p>
            A commonly quoted rule of thumb is around 4 to 6 cubic feet of refrigerator space per adult, adding more if you cook at home a lot or buy in bulk. By that rule, a couple might be comfortable with 14 to 18 cubic feet and a family of four with 20 to 25. Use it as a starting point, not a requirement.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Total Versus Fresh Food Capacity</h3>

          <p>
            The headline number is the total of the fresh food and freezer sections. Frigidaire's FFTR1835VW, for example, is 18.3 cubic feet in total, split into 13.4 cubic feet of fresh food and 4.9 of freezer. If you rarely use frozen food, compare the fresh food figure, not the total.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Why Layout Changes Usable Space</h3>

          <p>
            Drawers, door bins, ice makers and dispensers all take up space that the cubic feet figure still counts. A French door freezer drawer can be harder to pack fully than a top-freezer shelf, and an in-door ice maker can take up a large part of a fresh food door. Look at the interior photos, not just the number.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Bigger Is Not Always Better</h3>

          <p>
            A refrigerator that is too large for your household wastes energy cooling empty shelves and takes floor space you may want for other things. It can also force you into a wider or deeper cabinet than your kitchen was designed for. If you are between two sizes and the larger one only fits with no clearance to spare, choose the smaller model and keep a chest freezer or a second refrigerator in the garage or basement for overflow.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="door-swing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Door Swing and Clearance: The Measurement People Forget
          </h2>

          <p>
            A refrigerator that fits the opening can still be a problem if its doors cannot open fully or if it has no room to breathe. Door swing and clearance are the two measurements most often skipped.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Ventilation Clearance</h3>

          <p>
            Most spec sheets ask for a small gap around the cabinet. Whirlpool and Amana spec sheets, for example, suggest about half an inch at the sides and top and about an inch at the back. That space lets heat escape from the condenser, which helps the compressor and its lifespan; our guide to the <a href="/most-reliable-refrigerator-brands/">most reliable refrigerator brands</a> explains why that matters.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Door Swing Depth</h3>

          <p>
            An open door adds a lot of depth. Amana's spec sheet for the ART318FFD lists a depth of about 59 3/4 inches with the door open to 90 degrees. Measure from the back wall to the nearest island, wall or counter on the other side of the walkway, and make sure there is room to open the door and stand in front of it.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Hinge-Side Clearance</h3>

          <p>
            If a refrigerator sits right next to a wall, the door may not open far enough to pull out the crisper drawers. French doors and many full-width doors need some space on the hinge side to swing past 90 degrees. The installation guide lists how much; leave at least that.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Choosing a Style for a Tight Space</h3>

          <p>
            In a narrow kitchen, a side-by-side or French door has narrower doors that need less room in front. Many top-freezers and bottom-freezers have reversible doors, so you can choose which side the hinge goes on to suit the layout.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="measure" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. How to Measure Your Space and Doorways
          </h2>

          <p>
            Measure before you shop, not after the refrigerator arrives. A tape measure, a notepad and ten minutes save a failed delivery.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 1: Measure the Opening</h3>

          <p>
            Measure the width of the opening at the top, middle and bottom, and use the smallest number. Measure the height from the floor to the bottom of the cabinet above, again in more than one spot. Measure the depth from the back wall to the front edge of the counter.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 2: Subtract the Clearance</h3>

          <p>
            Take off the ventilation gaps the spec sheet asks for at the sides, top and back. The result is the largest refrigerator that will fit. Remember that outlets, water valves and baseboards can stick out from the back wall and reduce the depth.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 3: Measure Every Doorway and Hallway</h3>

          <p>
            Measure the width of every door, hallway and turn on the way into the kitchen. Many interior doors are around 30 to 32 inches wide, so a 36-inch French door may only fit through on its side or with its doors and handles removed. Check the spec sheet for the depth without doors.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 4: Check Stairs and Tight Turns</h3>

          <p>
            Stairs, landings and tight corners can be harder than doorways. Measure the clear height and width at each one and consider the refrigerator's diagonal, since it is often tilted onto a dolly.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 5: Confirm With the Spec Sheet</h3>

          <p>
            Before you order, download the spec sheet or installation guide for the exact model number and compare it with your notes. Our guide to the <a href="/best-refrigerator-brands/">best refrigerator brands</a> lists the dimensions of popular models across every style.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Measuring Only the Opening</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The kitchen opening is just one of the places the refrigerator has to fit. Doorways, hallways, stairs and tight turns are where deliveries most often fail. Measure the whole path from the street to the kitchen and compare each point with the refrigerator's depth without doors and handles.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Forgetting the Clearance Gaps</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A refrigerator that fills the opening exactly has nowhere to vent heat. Most spec sheets ask for a small gap at the sides and top and around an inch at the back. Subtract those gaps from your measurements first, then shop for a refrigerator that fits the space that is left.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Comparing Different Depth Measurements</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                One listing gives depth without doors, another with handles, a third with doors open. Comparing them directly makes a standard-depth model look counter-depth. Use the spec sheet and compare the same measurement across every model, ideally the depth with doors and handles.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Upgrading From 30 to 33 Inches Without Checking</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A 33-inch bottom-freezer will not slide into a 30-inch opening, and cabinets are rarely easy to move. If you want more capacity in the same space, look for a taller or deeper model at the same width, or accept a smaller step up in size.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring Door Swing</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A refrigerator door that hits an island, wall or dishwasher door makes the kitchen frustrating every day. Check the open-door depth on the spec sheet and the hinge-side clearance, and make sure you can open the door fully and still pull out the crisper drawers.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What are standard refrigerator dimensions?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Most full-size refrigerators are about 28 to 36 inches wide, 61 to 72 inches tall and 28 to 36 inches deep including doors and handles. Top-freezers are usually 30 inches wide and the shortest; French doors are usually 36 inches wide and the tallest. These are typical ranges, so always confirm the exact model's spec sheet before you buy.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the standard width of a refrigerator?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The most common widths are 30, 33 and 36 inches. Top-freezers and many bottom-freezers are 30 inches, many bottom-freezers are 33, and most French doors and side-by-sides are 36. Apartment models are often 24 inches and some top-freezers are 28. Measure your opening and buy a model slightly narrower than it.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How deep is a counter-depth refrigerator?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Counter-depth refrigerators are typically about 27 to 31 inches deep including the doors and handles, with a cabinet box closer to 24 or 25 inches. Standard-depth models are typically around 30 to 36 inches. Makers measure depth in different ways, so compare the same figure, such as depth with doors, across models.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much space do you need around a refrigerator?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Many spec sheets ask for about half an inch at the sides and top and about an inch at the back for ventilation. Some models need more, and doors may need extra room on the hinge side to open fully. Check the installation guide for your exact model and plan the opening with those gaps included.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How tall is a standard refrigerator?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Most top-freezers are about 61 to 69 inches tall, bottom-freezers about 67 to 70, and French doors and side-by-sides about 68 to 72. Some spec sheets list height to the hinge cap separately, which can add a fraction of an inch. If a cabinet sits above the opening, plan with the tallest listed figure plus the top clearance.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What size refrigerator do I need for a family of four?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A commonly cited rule of thumb is about 4 to 6 cubic feet per adult, which puts a family of four somewhere around 20 to 25 cubic feet. Households that cook a lot or buy in bulk may want more. Check that the larger refrigerator still fits your opening, doorways and walkway before choosing on capacity.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Will a refrigerator fit through a 30-inch door?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Many will, but not all. A refrigerator usually goes through a doorway on its depth, so compare the depth without doors and handles with the clear width of the doorway. Removing the doors and handles often gains several inches. Measure every doorway, hallway and turn on the delivery path, not just the kitchen opening.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

